<!-- TMost of this preview activity was written by Steven Clontz at the 2024 Doenet workshop -->

  <p>
    According to U.S. postal regulations, the size of a parcel sent by mail
    must satisfy certain constraints. Let's find the largest possible volume
    of a rectangular parcel with a square end that can be sent by U.S. mail.
  </p>

  <p>
    <ol marker="a.">
      <li>
        <p>
          Let <m>x</m> represent the length of one side of the square end and <m>y</m> the length of another edge of the box. Assume <m>x\lt y</m>.
          Label these quantities appropriately on the image below, and then check your work using the provided button.
        </p>
        <graph displayXAxis="false" displayYAxis="false" showNavigation="false" showBorder="false" aspectRatio="2" xmin="-2" xmax="8" ymin="-1" ymax="4" fixAxes="true"><shortDescription>sketch of the rectangular parcel with text entries for side lengths</shortDescription>
          <lineSegment draggable="false" endpoints="(0,0) (2,0)"/>
          <lineSegment draggable="false" endpoints="(2,0) (2,2)"/>
          <lineSegment draggable="false" endpoints="(2,2) (0,2)"/>
          <lineSegment draggable="false" endpoints="(0,2) (0,0)"/>
          <lineSegment draggable="false" endpoints="(2,0) (6,1)"/>
          <lineSegment draggable="false" endpoints="(2,2) (6,3)"/>
          <lineSegment draggable="false" endpoints="(0,2) (4,3)"/>
          <lineSegment draggable="false" endpoints="(4,3) (6,3)"/>
          <lineSegment draggable="false" endpoints="(6,1) (6,3)"/>
          <textInput draggable="false" width="15px" anchor="(1,-0.5)" name="squareLabel"><shortDescription>length of the shorter side</shortDescription></textInput>
          <textInput draggable="false" width="15px" anchor="(4,0)" name="depthLabel"><shortDescription>length of the longer side</shortDescription></textInput>
        </graph>
      <p><answer>
   <award>
     <when>$squareLabel = x and
       $depthLabel = y </when>
   </award>
 </answer></p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          State an expression involving <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> that gives the volume of the box (which is the quantity we wish to optimize).
        </p>
      <p>
         <answer name="toOptimize"><label><m>V = </m> </label>x^2y</answer>
      </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          The <q>girth</q> of the box is defined to be the perimeter of its square
side of the box.  According to U.S. mail regulations, the box's girth plus the length of its
other side cannot exceed <m>108</m> inches.
        </p>
        <p>
          State an equation involving <m>x</m> and <m>y</m> that is satisfied when the box's girth plus the length of its remaining side equals <m>108</m> inches.
        </p>
      <p>
        <answer name="girthEq" symbolicEquality simplifyOnCompare><shortDescription>equation for girth</shortDescription>4x+y=108</answer>
      </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Solve the equation
          <mathInput disabled><shortDescription>equation from part c</shortDescription><conditionalContent condition="$girthEq.creditAchieved=1">$girthEq</conditionalContent></mathInput>
          that you found in (c) for the variable <m>y</m>.
        </p>
      <p>
        <answer name="rewrite"><label><m>y=</m></label>108-4x</answer>
      </p>
      </li>

      <li>
        <p>
          Now, use your results from (a) and (d) to find a function
          <m>V(x)</m> of only the variable <m>x</m> whose output is the quantity
          to be optimized.
          <!--  where <m>y</m> is rewritten in terms of <m>x</m>
          according to <m>y=</m><mathInput disabled>$rewrite</mathInput> -->
        </p>
      <p>
        <answer><label><m>V(x)=</m></label>108x^2-4x^3</answer>
      </p>
      </li>

      <li sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
        <p>
          Next, we identify the domain on which to consider the volume function <m>V(x)</m> in the context of the model.
        </p>
        <p>
          The smallest value allowed for either <m>x</m> or <m>y</m> is zero;
          given the constraint <mathInput disabled><shortDescription>equation from part c</shortDescription><conditionalContent condition="$girthEq.creditAchieved=1">$girthEq</conditionalContent></mathInput> found in part (c),
          what is the largest value possible for <m>x</m>?
        </p>
      <p>
        <answer name="min"><label>The minimum possible value for <m>x</m> is </label>0</answer> and
         <answer name="max"><label>the maximum possible value for <m>x</m> is</label>108/4</answer>, so
        the <mathInput disabled minwidth="30"><label>domain of <m>V</m> in interval notation is</label>[<conditionalContent condition="$min.creditAchieved=1">$min</conditionalContent>,<conditionalContent condition="$max.creditAchieved=1">$max</conditionalContent>]</mathInput>.
      </p>
      </li>

      <li sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
        <p>
          The function <m>f(x)</m> has exactly one critical number that lies strictly between the endpoints of the domain <mathInput disabled minwidth="30"><shortDescription>domain of V in interval notation</shortDescription>[<conditionalContent condition="$min.creditAchieved=1">$min</conditionalContent>,<conditionalContent condition="$max.creditAchieved=1">$max</conditionalContent>]</mathInput>. We now find that critical number.
        </p>
        <p>
          By first finding that <answer><label><m>f'(x)=</m></label>216x-12x^2</answer>, and then setting <answer><label><m>f'(x) = </m> </label>0</answer> and solving for <m>x</m>, we find that the only critical number strictly between the endpoints of the domain is <answer name="critical"><label><m>x = </m></label>18</answer>.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
            Find the value of <m>f(x)</m> at each endpoints of its domain and this critical number.
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>V(</m> <mathInput disabled minWidth="20"><shortDescription>location of minimum</shortDescription><conditionalContent condition="$min.creditAchieved=1">$min</conditionalContent></mathInput><m>) =</m><answer><shortDescription>value of function at its minimum x value</shortDescription>0</answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>V(</m> <mathInput disabled minWidth="20"><shortDescription>location of maximum</shortDescription><conditionalContent condition="$max.creditAchieved=1">$max</conditionalContent></mathInput><m>) =</m><answer><shortDescription>value of function at its maximum x value</shortDescription>0</answer>
        </p>
        <p>
          <m>V(</m> <mathInput disabled minWidth="20"><shortDescription>location of critical point</shortDescription><conditionalContent condition="$critical.creditAchieved=1">$critical</conditionalContent></mathInput><m>) =</m><answer><shortDescription>value at the critical point</shortDescription>108*18^2-4*18^3</answer>
        </p>
      </li>
      <li sectionWideCheckWork forceIndividualAnswerColoring>
        In conclusion, we find that the box of largest possible volume that can be shipped has a square end where each side has length <answer><label><m>x = </m></label>18</answer> inches and the remaining side has length <answer><label><m>y = </m></label>36</answer>, and thus the maximum volume is <answer><label><m>V = </m></label>11664</answer> cubic inches.
      </li>
      <!--
      <li>
        <p>
          Describe the dimensions length <m>\times</m> width <m>\times</m> height, and overall volume, of the largest box that can be shipped via U.S. mail.  Then write a sentence or two explaining the calculus used to ensure that this value is optimized.
        </p>
        <p><answer type="text" handGraded expanded forceFullCheckworkButton /></p>
      </li>
      -->
    </ol>
  </p>